+ *triads*. The chord *d* | *f* + *a₁*, which in the usual musical notation is not distinguished from the minor triad *d₁* − *f* + *a₁*, and may hence be called the *false minor triad*, is, as Hauptmann has correctly shewn, dissonant, @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
and on justly-intoned instruments it is very decidedly dissonant. It sounds almost as rough as the chord *b₁* − *d* | *f*. If in *C* major, without confounding *d* with *d₁*, we form the cadence 1 or 2

[Figure m1]

@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
+ the chords *a₁'*...*d''* | *f''* and *f'* + *a₁'*...*d''* | *f''* are quite as dissonant in their effects as the following *b₁'* − *d''* | *f'* and *g'* + *b₁'* − *d''* | *f''*. @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
But on account of the incorrect intonation of our musical instruments we cannot produce the same effect ¶ without combining an inverted chord of the Seventh with the subdominant in the cadence, as *f* + *a₁* − *c'*...*d'*. @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
Hauptmann doubts whether in practice the false minor chord of the key of *C* major can be distinguished from the minor chord of *D*. I find that this is most distinctly and undoubtedly effected on my justly-intoned harmonium, @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
but allow that we cannot expect the correct intonation from singers. They will involuntarily pass into the minor chord, unless the progression of the parts which execute *D*, strongly emphasise its connection with the dominant *G*.⁎

@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
These chords, and among them most decisively and distinctly the chord *b₁* − *d* | *f*, have for musical composition the especially important advantage of combining those limiting tones of the key, which separate it from the nearest related keys, @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
and are consequently extremely well suited for marking the key in which the harmony is moving at any given time. If the harmony passed into *G* major or *G* minor, *f* would have to be replaced by *f₁*♯. @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
If it passed into *F* major *d* would become *d₁* and if into *F* minor *d* would become *d¹*♭ and *b₁* would in the ¶ same chords become *b¹*♭. Thus— @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[\begin{array}{lll} \text{in } G \text{ major}: & b_1 - d + f_1\sharp & d + f_1\sharp - a \\ \text{in } C \text{ major}: & b_1 - d \mid f & d \mid f + a_1 \\ \text{in } F \text{ major}: & b\flat + d_1 - f & d_1 - f + a_1 \\ @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\text{in } G \text{ minor}: & b^1\flat + d + f_1\sharp & d + f_1\sharp - a \\ \text{in } C \text{ minor}: & b_1 - d \mid f & d \mid f - a^1\flat \\ \text{in } F \text{ minor}: & b\flat - d^1\flat + f & d^1\flat + f - a^1\flat \end{array}\]

@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
This shews that the chords in the nearest related keys are all distinctly different, with the exception of *d* | *f* + *a₁* and *d₁* − *f* + *a₁*, the distinction between which in

